- name: note_0
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'GENERAL OVERVIEW - WHAT IS THIS?

      This is a WALKIE-TALKIE. The radio is not just a box, it is SOFTWARE.

      This is called SDR (Software Defined Radio): the antenna box (USRP)

      only receives and transmits signals through the air, while the "brain"

      that processes the audio is this flowgraph.

      Frequency: 462.5625 MHz (a real walkie-talkie channel).

      UPPER half = TALKING (microphone -> radio wave)

      LOWER half = LISTENING (radio wave -> speaker)

      The signal always flows from LEFT to RIGHT.'
    note: GENERAL OVERVIEW - WHAT IS THIS?
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1896, 56.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_0_0
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: ''
    note: Transmitter Side Flow
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [0, 624.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_0_1
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: ''
    note: Receiver Side Flow
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [32, 1056.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_1
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "CONTROL KNOBS (sliders appear on the screen when the program starts)\n\
      mic_gain        : microphone input volume level\nvolume_control  : speaker output\
      \ volume level\ntx_gain         : radio transmit power\nrx_gain         : radio\
      \ receive sensitivity\nptt             : push-to-talk. 1=transmits, 0=mutes\n\
      ch0_bandwidth   : bandwidth of the radio\u2019s hardware filter\ndirection (drc)\
      \ : switch that selects which channel is used for\n                  speaking\
      \ and which channel is used for listening (0 / 1)\nfreq_dev        : FM signal\
      \ deviation, meaning how much the frequency can vary"
    note: CONTROL KNOBS
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [24, 200.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_2
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "Audio Source: receives audio from the microphone\n48 kHz means 48,000\
      \ samples per second.\nHigh Pass Filter (300 Hz): removes low-frequency rumble\
      \ and deep background noise.\nLow Pass Filter (6 kHz): removes high-frequency\
      \ hiss and sharp noise.\nTogether, these filters leave mainly the human voice,\
      \ similar to telephone-quality speech.\nMic Gain (\xD7 mic_gain): adjusts the\
      \ microphone volume level.\nPush to Talk (\xD7 ptt):\nIf ptt = 1, the audio\
      \ passes through.\nIf ptt = 0, the audio is multiplied by zero and becomes silent.\n\
      This is the push-to-talk switch."
    note: "VOICE PATH \u2014 1) CLEAN THE AUDIO"
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2352, 720.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_3
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'WHY ARE THERE TWO BRANCHES? (channel 0 / channel 1)

      The radio has 2 channels. "direction" (drc) selects which one is active:

      one branch  x drc ,  the other branch  x (1 - drc)

      -> only ONE of them is active at the same time.

      This allows two radios to communicate without overlapping: while one radio

      transmits from channel 0, the other listens to it from channel 1.

      When the switch is changed, the roles are reversed.'
    note: WHY ARE THERE TWO BRANCHES?
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2360, 1032.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_4
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "WHAT IS NBFM?\nFM (Frequency Modulation) is used to carry audio through\
      \ the air\n\n* similar to FM radio, but NARROWBAND, which is walkie-talkie style.\n\
      \  NBFM Transmit : converts audio into an FM signal while transmitting\n  NBFM\
      \ Receive  : converts the FM signal back into audio while receiving\n  \"Audio\
      \ 48k / Quadrature 192k\": internally, the audio side is 48 kHz\n  and the radio\
      \ signal side is 192 kHz."
    note: WHAT IS NBFM?
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2352, 48.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_5
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'RATIONAL RESAMPLER = RATE CONVERTER (like a gearbox)

      Each part works at a different rate: audio is 48k, the FM side is 192k, and
      the radio (USRP) is 1 MHz.

      This block matches these rates to each other:

      While transmitting : 192,000 x (125/24) = 1,000,000  (goes UP to the radio rate)

      While receiving    : 1,000,000 x (24/125) = 192,000  (goes DOWN to the FM rate)

      Interpolation = multiply / speed up,  Decimation = divide / slow down.'
    note: RATIONAL RESAMPLER = RATE CONVERTER (like a gearbox)
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2352, 272.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_6
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'LISTENING PATH (bottom row, left to right)


      1. USRP Source: receives the raw radio signal from the antenna (1 MHz).

      2. Low Pass + Resampler + NBFM Receive: filters it and converts it back into
      audio.

      3. Band Pass Filter (300-6000 Hz): again, passes only the speech range.

      4. direction multiplier -> Add: combines the ACTIVE one of the 2 channels into
      a single audio signal.

      5. x ptt: cuts the audio so you do not hear your own transmission while talking.

      6. x volume_control -> Audio Sink: sends the audio to the speaker (48 kHz).'
    note: LISTENING PATH (bottom row, left to right)
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2352, 480.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_7
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'DISPLAYS (bottom right) - visual only, they do not affect the audio

      QT GUI Waterfall Sink : shows frequencies over time with colors, like a waterfall.

      QT GUI Frequency Sink : shows the spectrum of the current signal, meaning the
      frequency graph.

      Both are connected to the receive output and allow you to SEE the signal.'
    note: DISPLAY
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [576, 224.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_8
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'When using one USRP, one channel can transmit while the other channel
      can receive.

      If you change the direction value, the roles are swapped:

      the previously receiving channel starts transmitting, and the previously transmitting
      channel starts receiving.

      If you want to listen from another USRP, disable the upper part and activate
      the lower part.

      Be careful with the addresses. Each USRP has a different address value, and
      you must set it manually.

      If you are only on the receiver side, changing transmitter-side parameters will
      not affect anything because the transmitter part, which is the upper part of
      the flowgraph, is disabled.'
    note: General Usage
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1200, 192.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
